{"causes": ["The catastrophic Roman defeat at the Battle of Cannae in 216 BCE necessitated exceptional leadership to salvage surviving forces and maintain morale.", "The ongoing Second Punic War demanded experienced military commanders and diplomats capable of managing multiple fronts and securing Roman alliances.", "Rome's burgeoning geopolitical interests in the Hellenistic East required diplomatic engagement to counter the influence of powers like Macedon and protect its Adriatic flank."], "effects": ["Publius Sempronius Tuditanus's successful retreat from Cannae in 216 BCE provided a vital morale boost and preserved a contingent of Roman fighting men.", "The Treaty of Phoenice, negotiated by Tuditanus in 205 BCE, concluded the First Macedonian War, allowing Rome to focus entirely on the Second Punic War against Hannibal.", "His diplomatic engagement with Philip V of Macedon established a precedent for Roman intervention in Hellenistic affairs, paving the way for future Roman dominance in Greece and Macedon."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "second-punic-war", "sourceName": "Second Punic War", "verb": "OCCURS_DURING", "targetSlug": "publius-sempronius-tuditanus", "targetName": "Publius Sempronius Tuditanus", "context": "Publius Sempronius Tuditanus's entire career, from his military tribune service to his consulship and diplomatic missions, occurred during the Second Punic War (218–201 BCE)."}, {"sourceSlug": "battle-of-cannae", "sourceName": "Battle of Cannae", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "publius-sempronius-tuditanus", "targetName": "Publius Sempronius Tuditanus", "context": "Publius Sempronius Tuditanus participated in the Battle of Cannae in 216 BCE, where he famously led approximately 600 Roman survivors to safety."}, {"sourceSlug": "publius-sempronius-tuditanus", "sourceName": "Publius Sempronius Tuditanus", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "treaty-of-phoenice", "targetName": "Treaty of Phoenice", "context": "Publius Sempronius Tuditanus negotiated and signed the Treaty of Phoenice in 205 BCE, officially ending the First Macedonian War."}, {"sourceSlug": "publius-sempronius-tuditanus", "sourceName": "Publius Sempronius Tuditanus", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "rome-italy", "targetName": "Rome, Italy", "context": "Publius Sempronius Tuditanus held various magistracies, including consul and censor, within the political system centered in Rome, Italy."}, {"sourceSlug": "philip-v-of-macedon", "sourceName": "Philip V of Macedon", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "publius-sempronius-tuditanus", "targetName": "Publius Sempronius Tuditanus", "context": "Philip V of Macedon collaborated with Publius Sempronius Tuditanus to negotiate the terms of the Treaty of Phoenice in 205 BCE."}], "places": [{"name": "Rome, Italy", "role": "Political Center"}, {"name": "Cannae, Italy", "role": "Battle Site"}, {"name": "Phoenice, Epirus", "role": "Treaty Location"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Publius Sempronius Tuditanus", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "publius-sempronius-tuditanus"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q935520", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Publius_Sempronius_Tuditanus"], "tags": ["Political Leaders", "political-systems", "geopolitical-linkage", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Publius Sempronius Tuditanus significantly impacted Roman foreign policy by negotiating the Treaty of Phoenice, which disengaged Rome from the First Macedonian War and allowed it to concentrate on Hannibal, directly contributing to Rome's eventual victory in the Second Punic War. His leadership at Cannae also provided a crucial example of Roman resilience in the face of overwhelming defeat.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-08T00:45:50Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#28909017767", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Publius Sempronius C.f. Tuditanus was a Roman Republican consul and censor, best known for leading about 600 men to safety at Cannae in August, 216 BC and for the Treaty of Phoenice which ended the First Macedonian War, in 205 BC.", "newValue": "Publius Sempronius Tuditanus (born 242 BCE, died 200 BCE) was a distinguished Roman Republican statesman and military commander whose career spanned the tumultuous Second Punic War and its immediate aftermath, a period that fundamentally reshaped Rome's position in the Mediterranean. His strategic acumen and diplomatic skill were instrumental in securing Roman survival during its darkest hour, most notably through his leadership at Cannae, and later in formalizing Rome's emerging dominance in the Hellenistic East via the Treaty of Phoenice. He exemplified the Roman senatorial elite who transitioned the Republic from a regional power to a burgeoning empire.

Tuditanus first achieved renown in August 216 BCE, during the catastrophic Battle of Cannae, where he, then a military tribune, led approximately 600 Roman survivors through Carthaginian lines to safety, a rare act of successful resistance amidst overwhelming defeat. His political ascent saw him elected aedile in 209 BCE, praetor in 208 BCE, and consul in 204 BCE, where he campaigned against Hannibal in Bruttium. His most enduring diplomatic contribution came in 205 BCE, when, as proconsul, he negotiated the Treaty of Phoenice with Philip V of Macedon, ending the First Macedonian War and securing Roman strategic interests in the Balkans and Greece without committing extensive resources.

His actions at Cannae provided a crucial morale boost and practical example of resilience for a shattered Rome, proving that even in utter defeat, Roman soldiers could fight their way to safety. The Treaty of Phoenice, while seemingly a minor peace, strategically disengaged Rome from a secondary front, allowing full concentration on Hannibal, and established a precedent for Roman intervention in Hellenistic affairs that would define the next century of Mediterranean power politics. It laid the groundwork for future Roman military and diplomatic expansion into Greece and Macedon, directly contributing to Rome's eventual hegemony."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-08T00:45:50Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#28909017767", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "242 BCE, http://www.wikidata.org/.well-known/genid/f36ed3359c1e216b540a41bf8e527603"}, {"title": "Political crises and power transitions requiring new leadership", "type": "Idea", "year": "242 BCE, http://www.wikidata.org/.well-known/genid/f36ed3359c1e216b540a41bf8e527603"}], "newValue": ["The catastrophic Roman defeat at the Battle of Cannae in 216 BCE necessitated exceptional leadership to salvage surviving forces and maintain morale.", "The ongoing Second Punic War demanded experienced military commanders and diplomats capable of managing multiple fronts and securing Roman alliances.", "Rome's burgeoning geopolitical interests in the Hellenistic East required diplomatic engagement to counter the influence of powers like Macedon and protect its Adriatic flank."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-08T00:45:50Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#28909017767", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Shaped political institutions and governance practices", "type": "Idea", "year": "200 BCE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional f